  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 230 AM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nFriday night into Saturday a weak tropical wave will advect\nthrough the Straits and result in an increase in moisture across\nall of south Florida. There's still some uncertainty in the\ntiming and subsequently how much rain we receive. A more \nnocturnal passage would likely limit the intensity of storms, \nwhile a slower passage could mean that the moisture arrives during\npeak instability on Saturday afternoon. Either way, Friday night \ninto Saturday has the potential to be wet. Late Saturday and \nthrough Sunday a strong SAL will overspread and preclude any \nconvection. Early next week, easterly flow will remain intact, and\nas mentioned above moisture will slowly increase. This will \nlikely result in a gradual increase in convective coverage each \nday, and mostly focused across the interior and southwest FL.\n\nHeat advisory conditions will persist this weekend through next\nweek for at least portions of south Florida. We'll likely get the\nlowest dewpoints on Sunday, but the low-level airmass will be at\nits warmest and most likely offset and moisture reduction as it\nrelates to apparent temperatures. As moisture returns early next\nweek and the upper ridge remains anomalously strong, heat indices\nwill likely exceed 105-108 each day.",
